  "decision_conditions": [
    "The development to which this permission relates must be begun not later than the expiration of three years beginning with the date of the decision notice. \rReason: To comply with the provisions of S.51 of the Planning & Compulsory Purchase Act 2004.\r",
    "The development hereby permitted shall be carried out in accordance with the following approved plans:\r\rE01-E04\rP01-P07\r\rReason: For the avoidance of doubt and in the interests of proper planning.\r",
    "Unless otherwise specified in the application the materials to be used for the external surfaces of the development hereby permitted along with work of making good shall be carried out in materials to match those of the existing building.\r\rReason: To ensure that the appearance of the development respects the appearance, existing materials and built and natural features of the surrounding area.\r",
    "Prior to occupation, unless otherwise agreed, cycle parking should be provided in compliance with the following criteria:\r\r-\tProvision of a minimum of SIX long stay cycle parking spaces, one per HMO bedroom \r-\tIn an accessible location. The access route to the cycle parking enclosures should be a minimum of 1.2 metres wide to enable the bike to enter the store. It should be step free and involve no more than two doors or gates, excluding the store door, which must also have a minimum opening width of 1.2 metres.\r-\tLong stay must be secured with a British Standard mortice lock or access fob.\r-\tLong stay must be fully enclosed with four walls and a roof.\r\rReason: The Council's Climate Action and Transport Policies seek to increase the use of active and sustainable transport modes. This means that access to bicycle parking should be easy and secure to remove potential barriers to cycling.\r",
    "The development hereby permitted shall not be occupied until waste storage and recycling facilities to satisfy the future number of occupiers on site have been provided within the development, in accordance with Enfield's Household Recycling and Waste Planning Guidance for New Developments (February 2024). Such facilities shall be retained permanently thereafter.\r\rReason: To safeguard the amenity of the occupiers of adjoining premises; protect the general environment; prevent any obstruction to pedestrian movement and ensure that there are adequate facilities for the storage and recycling of recoverable materials.\r",
    "No independent cooking or laundry facilities, including hot plates, shall be installed in any of the respective bedrooms of the development hereby permitted and the communal kitchen,dining and living room shall be retained in perpetuity. \r\rReason: To accord with the stipulations of definition as a House of Multiple Occupation and to safeguard the residential character of the surrounding area.\r",
    "The use of the property as a House in Multiple Occupation hereby approved shall be occupied by a maximum of six people at any one time and shall not be subdivided or occupied as self-contained units. \r\rReason: To accord with the stipulations of definition as a House of Multiple Occupation and to safeguard the residential character of the surrounding area.\r"
  ],
